We introduce a novel random dynamical system (RDS) characterized by a pair of functions, $(\phi, \psi)$, which govern the evolution of a vector field $(u,v)$ via the system:
\begin{equation*}
\left\{
\begin{aligned}
\dot u(t)&=\phi(u(t),v(t)) + \psi(u(t),v(t))\circ W(t)\\
\dot v(t)&=\psi(u(t),v(t))\circ W(t)
\end{aligned}
\right.
\end{equation*}
where $W$ represents standard Brownian motion. We demonstrate that, provided $\phi$ and $\psi$ satisfy specific conditions, this system generates an RDS featuring both drifting and swirling components. Furthermore, we establish the existence of an ergodic measure for this RDS. Additionally, we illustrate that many well-known phenomena observed in plasma physics can be modeled using this framework. Finally, we explore methods for incorporating friction and diffusion terms into the equations, analyzing the resulting implications for randomness and ergodicity. To support these findings, we include numerical simulations of the examples discussed. Keywords: Random Dynamical Systems, Ergodic Theory, Ergodic Measure, Ergodic Differential Equations, Ergodic Differential Systems, Ergodic Systems, Ergodic Theory, Ergodicity, Nonlinear Dynamics, Nonlinear Systems, Probability and Statistics, Probability Theory, Stochastic Processes. 2010 Mathematics Subject Classification: 37A25, 37L30, 37L45, 37L55, 37L50, 37L60, 37L65, 37L70, 37L80, 37L90, 37L95, 37L98, 37M15, 37N15, 37N20, 37N25, 37N30, 37N35, 37N40, 37N50, 37N55, 37N60, 37N90, 37N95, 37L40, 37L45, 37L50, 37L55, 37L60

